course: politics
lesson: 12
title: Adverts, slogans and polls
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Before an election, candidates try to get noticed the way a new cereal does: adverts, catchy slogans, posters, signs on lawns and big cheering rallies. All of it is a shop window that shows only the best side.
explain: A slogan is like the cover of a book: three words you cannot forget, while the real promises sit inside a long document called a manifesto.
explain: Polls are different: they are not selling, they are guessing. A poll asks one or two thousand people, picked to look like the whole country, and gives a guess with a wobble, like tasting one spoon to judge a whole pot of soup.
explain: Signs, crowds and lamppost faces tell you who is loud, not who will win, because the vote is counted one person at a time.
explain: People talk about all this in their own words: in Australia a "servo" is a petrol station and the "arvo" is the afternoon; in Canada a "loonie" is a one-dollar coin; and an older American might say a sunny advert is "groovy" and a lost election a "bummer".
fact: Campaign adverts are paid for by the candidate or by groups who support them, and in many countries the advert must say who paid.
fact: Some say adverts are part of free speech; others want limits on spending so rich groups cannot drown out everyone else.
fact: Yard signs show which candidate a household supports, but they do not tell you who will win, because most people never put one up.
fact: A slogan is a short catchy phrase; the full list of promises is in a party's manifesto, which most people never read.
fact: An attack ad criticises the other candidate instead of praising your own, often with the worst photo and gloomy music; this is called negative campaigning.
fact: An opinion poll asks around one or two thousand people chosen to match the country by age, place and more; it gives a good guess, not a sure answer.
fact: The margin of error is the wobble, how far off a poll could be just by chance, so when two parties are very close in a poll, the gap may mean nothing.
fact: A poll is a snapshot of today: people change their minds, some do not answer, and some who say they will vote never do; turnout means how many actually vote.
fact: Some countries ban new polls in the last days before voting.
fact: Canvassing means volunteers going door to door to talk to voters and hear what they care about.
fact: In Australia voters number the candidates in order; parties hand out how-to-vote cards, but voters may number the boxes any way they like.
fact: Rallies fire up supporters; crowd sizes are hard to count and often argued about, and camera angles can make a crowd look bigger or smaller.
fact: In Ireland candidates must take their election posters down within a week after the vote or they can be fined.
exercise: Who pays for a campaign advert? | The candidate or groups who support them | The TV channel, for free | The voters who watch it | The cartoon makers
exercise: Why must many adverts say who paid for them? | So you can tell who is trying to win your vote | So the advert lasts longer | So children can skip it | So it counts as news
exercise: What do yard signs on a street tell you? | Which candidate some households support | Who will win the election in the whole of the country | How many people in the street will go and vote on the day | The full plan of each party, written out in detail
exercise: What is a manifesto? | The written list of what a party promises to do | A three-word catchy phrase that a party puts on every poster it makes | A poster on a lamppost | A survey of voters
exercise: What is an attack ad? | An advert criticising the other candidate | An advert praising your own candidate and all their plans | A news report about a debate shown on the TV news | A poll result shown on TV as if it were an advert
exercise: Roughly how many people does a national opinion poll usually ask? | About one or two thousand | Every single voter | About ten people | Only the candidates
exercise: How does a poll pick people? | To match the whole country by age, place and more | Only people who answer the adverts on TV and phone the number on the screen | Only people at rallies | Only people with yard signs
exercise: A poll says 38 percent against 35. What should you remember? | The gap may be too small to mean much | One party has certainly won | The poll asked everybody | Polls are never close
exercise: What is turnout? | How many people actually vote | How many posters are put up | How loud a rally is | How many polls are taken
exercise: Why do some countries ban new polls just before voting day? | They worry polls may change how people vote | Polls are always wrong in the last week before the vote | Pollsters all go away on holiday before voting day | Voters are not allowed to read any polls by then
exercise: What is canvassing? | Going door to door to talk to voters | Painting posters for a party | Counting votes after polling day | Buying adverts on YouTube
exercise: In Australia, what does a how-to-vote card give you? | A party's suggested order for numbering the boxes | A rule from the law that every single voter must follow | Your own ticket to get you into the polling station | The final result of the count, printed early for voters
exercise: Why are crowd sizes at rallies often argued about? | They are hard to count and camera angles can mislead | Every crowd is counted exactly, one by one, by the police at the gate as people walk in | Rallies are always empty | Only police may see them
exercise: In Ireland, how soon must election posters come down after the vote? | Within a week | Within a full year | Never, they stay up | Before the vote starts
exercise: What does a big rally crowd show? | Excited supporters, not how most voters will choose | That the candidate has already won the election before any vote is counted | That every voter agrees | That the polls are wrong
exercise: What does a slogan usually leave out? | The detailed promises about money, schools and hospitals | The party's name and the name of its leader, written in big letters | Any catchy words | A short phrase
test: Mateo's mate: "our street's 9 signs to 4, so our guy's def winning the whole state". What is wrong? | A street of signs is not a vote count; most people never put one up | Nothing, signs always predict the winner, because every family that votes puts one up on the lawn | Signs count as real votes | Only the state with most signs votes
test: "That advert with the golden dog was groovy, man, I'm sold" says Grandpa (groovy = great). What is the wisest reply? | An advert shows only what the candidate wants you to see | Adverts must be fully balanced by law, so it shows the good and bad sides of the candidate | The dog proves the candidate is kind | Adverts are made by neutral reporters
test: A Melbourne voter: "the bloke at the school gate gave me a card, so I gotta number 'em his way, yeah?" What is true? | No, the card is only a suggestion and the vote is his choice | Yes, it is the law to follow the card from the bloke at the gate, or you pay a fine | Yes, or the vote is not counted | No, cards are only for children
test: Spooky music, a grey photo and a deep voice say a candidate "can't be trusted". What should you ask? | What real evidence is there behind it? | Which song is playing? | Why is the photo grey? | Who is the narrator?
test: "Nobody asked me, so the poll's rubbish innit" says Fatima's cousin. What is the best answer? | Polls ask a smaller group picked to look like the whole country | Polls must ask every single person in the country, or the result does not count at all | Polls only ask politicians | Polls are made up without asking anyone
test: What would happen if a poll asked only people at one party's rally? | It would give a wrong picture, because they are not like the whole country | It would be the most accurate poll possible, because those people care most about the election | It would match the result exactly | It would show the other party winning
test: "Polls were well off last time, so this one's pants" (pants = rubbish). What is fair to say? | Polls are a snapshot of today and can miss, but are usually a fair guess | Polls are always exactly right, so last time the voters must have changed their minds on purpose | Polls are never close to the result | Polls decide the election
test: A volunteer with a clipboard knocks in Winnipeg: "Got a sec, eh? What matters most to ya?" What can Dad do? | Chat, or ask the volunteer a question back | He must say who he will vote for | He must sign up to the party | He must buy a leaflet
test: Unc is wicked sure: "crowd was 100,000, easy!" (wicked = very, in New England). The news reader says far fewer. What is true? | Crowd sizes are hard to count and often argued about | The biggest number said is always right, because the people who were there saw it | The news reader counted each person | Crowds are measured by the loudest chant
test: Amira, Dublin: "your man's face is on every pole, who takes 'em all down?" (your man = that man) | The candidates, within a week after the vote, or they can be fined | Nobody, they stay up forever on the poles until the wind and rain take them down | The voters who liked him | The other parties, for free
test: A party's slogan is three catchy words. Ruby's teacher asks what it actually promises. Where should Ruby look? | In the party's manifesto | On the party's posters only | In the attack ads about the party | In a poll about the party
exam: Identify the purpose of the statement "paid for by" which is required to appear in campaign advertisements in many jurisdictions. | To inform the viewer of the person or body which financed the advertisement | To certify that the advertisement is accurate in every respect and has been checked by the courts | To indicate that the advertisement constitutes impartial news | To announce the result of the forthcoming election
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an opinion poll? | An estimate derived from a sample selected to resemble the population, subject to a margin of error | A complete count of the intentions of every eligible voter, conducted by the electoral commission before polling day | A binding vote which determines the result of the election | An advertisement financed by a candidate
exam: It is given that, in an Australian election, a party distributes a card indicating a preferred order of candidates. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The card constitutes a suggestion, and the voter may number the boxes in any order | The voter is obliged by law to follow the card, on pain of a fine imposed by the electoral commission | Any ballot departing from the card shall be discarded | The card is issued by the electoral commission
exam: Ascertain the period within which candidates in Ireland are required to remove their election posters following the poll. | Within one week | Within one full year | Within one single hour | Prior to the poll itself
